{
  "title": "Triangular Fibrocartilage Complex (TFCC) Injury",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/hand/6047/triangular-fibrocartilage-complex-tfcc-injury",
  "subtitle": "Updated: Dec 18 2023",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "Summary",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Triangular Fibrocartilage Complex (TFCC) Injuries, a common cause of ulnar-sided wrist pain, may result from trauma or due to degenerative changes.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Diagnosis is made clinically with ulnar sided wrist pain that is worse with ulnar deviation and a positive \"fovea\" sign. An MRI can help confirm diagnosis.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Treatment is generally conservative with NSAIDs and immobilization. Surgical debridement, TFCC repair or ulnar shortening procedures may be indicated depending on severity of symptoms and underlying cause.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Etiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Mechanism of TFCC injury",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Type 1 traumatic injury",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "mechanism",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "most common is fall on **extended wrist with forearm pronation**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "traction injury to ulnar side of wrist",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "3",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "traction injury to ulnar wrist",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "Type 2 degenerative injury",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "associated with **positive ulnar variance**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "associated with ulnocarpal impaction",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Anatomy",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "TFCC made up of",
          "table": null,
          "images": [
            {
              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/6047/images/anatomic%20illustration.jpg",
              "caption": "Shows elements of TFCC"
            }
          ],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "dorsal and volar radioulnar ligaments",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "deep lig, known as **ligamentum subcruentum, attach to the ulnar fovea**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "superficial fibers attach to the ulnar styloid",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "central articular disc",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "meniscus homolog",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "ulnar collateral ligament",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "ECU subsheath",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "origin of ulnolunate and ulnotriquetral ligaments",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Blood supply",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "periphery is well vascularized (10-40% of the periphery)",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**central portion is avascular**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Origin",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "dorsal and volar radioulnar ligaments originate at the sigmoid notch of the radius",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Insertion",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "5",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "dorsal and volar radioulnar ligaments converge at the base of the ulnar styloid",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/6047/images/ulnar_fovea..jpg",
                  "caption": "Ulnar fovea anatomy"
                }
              ],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "deep fibers insert on to the ulnar fovea",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "superficial fibers insert on the ulnar styloid",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
